    <DataNode elementId="CPD-13636" textLabel="(12Z)-9,10-epoxyocta-dec-12-enoate" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="FBUKMFOXMZRGRB-YFHOEESVSA-M" dataSource="InChIKey" />
      <Graphics centerX="200" centerY="1080" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="AU4FS-4, SRI, caspi, O-28, pujar">|FRAME: CPD-13636| is an epoxy unsaturated fatty acid. When studied in plants, it is usually referred to as &quot;coronaric acid&quot;. When studied in mammals, it is named &quot;leukotoxin&quot;. The compound has been found in seed oils derived from plants in the sunflower family such as |FRAME: TAX-4232| |CITS: [17805802]| and |FRAME: TAX-145993| |CITS: [17805745]|. |FRAME: CPD-13636| is also produced in mammals in neutrophlls as a defensive response to infection. It shows antifungal and antibacterial activity. The compound is produced by oxygenation of |FRAME: LINOLEIC_ACID| by |FRAME: HS06458-MONOMER| and probably other CYPs that metabolize polyunsaturated fatty acids to epoxides |CITS: [10729206][20869469][25093613]|. The same enzymes, when acting on the other double bond of |FRAME: LINOLEIC_ACID|, form |FRAME: Vernolates|, also known as isoleukotoxin. Over-production of leukotoxin, which is associated with severe infection, results in the genesis of coagulating abnormalities |CITS: [3947358][3345035][3398695][2384990]|. Leukotoxin has also been associated with the multiple organ failure and adult respiratory distress syndrome seen in some severe burn patients |CITS: [7862104]|. It has been shown that leukotoxin itself is not toxic. Rather, it is acted upon by soluble epoxide hydrolase, and the resulting diol, |FRAME: CPD-24201|, is the toxic form |CITS: [9142128]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="CPD-13636" />
